title: git-tree.awk
---
date: 2026-09-22
---
body:

I wrote a git tree visualiser in AWK. Here it is showing the tree for [accent.vim](https://github.com/Alligator/accent.vim), my vim coloursceheme:

[![](screen.png)](screen.png)

You can try it [here](/static/accent.html).

The source code is [here](https://github.com/Alligator/git-tree.awk).

It generates a single static HTML file. The file tree is on the left, the file content is on the right. Clicking on a file will show its content.

I learned some interesting things about AWK and HTML by writing this, but first.

## Why?

For fun, and because I like AWK. It's minimal, it's everywhere, it's older than me (and probably you). Writing programs in AWK is like being a beginner again; you have a limited set of tools and you have to find new ways to solve problems with them.

----

Here are some things I learned about AWK.

## Multidimensional Arrays

I store a type (`blob` or `tree`) and a path for each file in the repo. In another language I'd keep a list of objects:

```javascript
[
  { type: "blob", path: "README.md" },
  { type: "tree", path: "src" },
  { type: "blob", path: "src/main.go" },
]
```

But AWK only has one collection type, the associative array. [POSIX AWK](https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/awk.html) doesn't support nested arrays either. Unless I want to reply on GNU AWK (I don't), I can't do this:

```awk
files[i]["type"] = type
files[i]["path"] = path
```

You *can* use comma separated indexes though:

```awk
files[i,"type"] = type
files[i,"path"] = path
```

<aside>
  <p>
    There are lots of different AWKs. BSD-derived systems including macOS come with nawk, also known as the One True AWK as it's maintained by Brian Kernighan (the K in AWK) and others.
  </p>
  <p>
  GNU AWK (or gawk) is also common and, as you'd expect from the FSF, has many, many, <em>many</em> extra features.
  </p>
</aside>

This creates an index by joining all the expressions together with `SUBSEP`. On my system AWK (nawk) `SUBSEP` is 28, or the ASCII file separator character

This does make the array longer than the number of files since there's two entries per file. I store the number of files in a separate variable instead.

## nawk doesn't like 0 bytes in strings

When a file is binary, I show "binary file" instead of it's content. I decided on a *very* rough heuristic for this: if there's a 0 byte in the first 100 bytes of a file, it's binary. My plan was to read a few lines with `getline` and call `index()` to check for a 0 byte:

```awk
if (index(line, "\0") > 0) {
  # file is binary
}
```

But this call to `index` always returned 1, and every file was considered binary. Here's a small program that hints at why:

```shell
$ awk 'BEGIN {
  print "one two\0three"
  print index("one\0two", "\0")
  print length("\0")
}'
one two
1
0
```

nawk will parse `\0` in a string and insert a 0 byte, but also treats it as a string terminator! This is very confusing if you're not expecting it.

My `index` call always returned 1 because it's actually checking this:

```awk
index(line, "")
```

And now a digression about AWK version differences.

----

I tried this across a few AWKs:

AWK | `length("\0")` | `index("a\0b", "\0")`
----|---------
nawk | 0 | 1
GNU AWK | 1 | 2
busybox AWK | 0 | 0
mawk | 1 | 2
GoAWK | 1 | 2

Note that `index` returns 1 for the first character in the string.

GNU AWK, mawk and GoAWK get it right, nawk and busybox AWK don't.

The same null-termination problem happens when you read data from files, stdin, or via `getline`. mawk will truncate the string. The well behaved AWKs all read the whole string with the zero byte in it. Busybox AWK is weird. If I create a file with this content:

```
one\0two
```

Then use it as the input file, this happens:

```shell
$ busybox awk '{ print $0 }' zerosep.txt
one
two
```

The zero byte was treated as a record separator, apparently.

----

Anyway, I couldn't find a way to check a string for a null byte in nawk, so instead I use `od` to dump the bytes and check for `00`:

```awk
function is_binary(name) {
  while (("od -N 100 -v -t x1 -A n " name | getline l) > 0) {
    if (index(l, "00") > 0) {
      return 1
    }
  }
  return 0
}
```

## `getline` and `close()` are weird

To measure how long the program takes, I store timestamps and calculate the difference. I first did this:

```awk
"date +%s" | getline start_ts
# ... rest of program
"date +%s" | getline end_ts
diff_ts = end_ts - start_ts
```

But `end_ts` was always empty, unless I commented out the `start_ts` line. Printing the result of `getline` gives us some clues why:

```shell
$ awk 'BEGIN {
  print ("date +%s" | getline start_ts)
  print ("date +%s" | getline end_ts)
}'
1
0
```

`1` means success, `0` means end of file.

The first `getline` executes `date` and sets up a pipe, the next `getline` is reading from the `same` pipe. The `date` command is finished after one line, so there's no more output.

That seems obvious in retrospect, but it wasn't at all at the time.

To close the pipe and run a new command, you have to call `close()`, with the *literal string* you used in the first call. So to fix my bug, I call `close("date +%s")` after getting each timestamp.

I mean the literal string too, whitespace differences will run a new command:

```shell
$ awk 'BEGIN {
  "date" | getline date1
  " date" | getline date2
  print date1
  print date2
}'
Wed 23 Sep 2026 14:35:44 BST
Wed 23 Sep 2026 14:35:44 BST
```

----

Here are some things I learned about HTML and CSS by making the page interactive with no JavaScript.

## The `<details>` element

I already knew about the [`<details>` element](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/details), but it's an excellent addition to HTML and I want to remind you it exists. It creates an expandable section with a label (set by the `<summary>` tag).

I used it to create directories which can be expanded and collapsed:

![](docs-collapsed.png)
![](docs-expanded.png)
<br/><small>
The docs directory collapsed and expanded.
</small>

## The `:target` pseudo-class

When a file is selected on the left, I show the file's content on the right. How do we do that without JavaScript? First, each file is rendered as a `<section>` element with an id taken from its index in the file array:

```html
<section id="file-0">
  <h2>.gitignore</h2>
  <pre>
    *.json
    build/
  </pre>
</section>
```

Then I hide them all with this CSS:

```css
.content > section {
  display: none;
}
```

Showing the selected file involves, two parts. The files in the tree are anchor links with an id that matches the relevant section:

```html
<a href="#file-0">.gitignore</a>
<a href="#file-1">LICENSE</a>
<a href="#file-2">README.md</a>
```

This is the magic CSS to un-hide the file content:

```css
.content > section:target {
  display: block;
}
```

The `:target` pseudo class selects the element the URL fragment points to. If the URL is `repo.html#file-1`, the section with the id `file-1` becomes the target and is un-hidden.

----

That's it. If you've never tried writing more than a one-liner in AWK I recommend it. It's familiar, but odd.

## Should you use it?

Probably not.
